course: everyday
lesson: 85
title: Splinters, stings, allergies and asthma
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Splinters, stings and itchy plants are mostly small troubles, but the right first aid matters: wash, do not rub, keep calm and tell an adult.
explain: An allergy is when the body's defences react strongly to something that is harmless for most people, like peanuts, pollen or a bee sting; a severe reaction, called anaphylaxis, can kill quickly.
explain: Asthma narrows the tubes that carry air into the lungs, so breathing is hard and wheezy; a reliever inhaler opens them again.
explain: At a party in Auckland a boy with a peanut allergy tells the host's parent before eating, reads the label on the biscuits, and has a safe, happy afternoon.
fact: A splinter should be removed by an adult with clean tweezers and the skin washed, because a splinter left in can get infected.
fact: When a wasp flies near you, stay calm and move slowly away, because swatting makes a sting more likely.
fact: If a spider bites you in Australia, keep still and tell an adult, because some Australian spiders are dangerous and need first aid.
fact: If a jellyfish stings you, get out of the water, tell a lifeguard or adult and rinse with seawater, not fresh water; in tropical Australia, such as northern Queensland, vinegar is used; weeing on a sting is a myth that does not help.
fact: After touching stinging nettles, wash the skin and do not rub, because rubbing spreads the sting.
fact: In the USA and Canada, a rash after touching a plant may be poison ivy, so wash with soap and water and tell an adult.
fact: Never share food with a classmate who has a peanut allergy, and wash your hands after eating peanuts, because even a tiny amount can make them very ill.
fact: Swelling, hives (itchy raised bumps on the skin), coughing or trouble breathing in a person with an allergy may be anaphylaxis: get an adult at once and call the emergency number.
fact: An adrenaline auto-injector can save the life of someone having a severe allergic reaction, so know where your friend keeps it.
fact: If you have an allergy, always tell adults at parties and friends' houses and read food labels.
fact: Hay fever is an allergy to pollen; keeping windows closed on high pollen days and washing your face after being outside helps.
fact: In an asthma attack, sit the person up, help them use their reliever inhaler and fetch an adult.
exercise: You get a splinter in your finger. What is best? | An adult removes it with clean tweezers and you wash it | Leave it in for a few weeks until it comes out on its own | Push it further in so it disappears | Bite it out with your teeth
exercise: A wasp is buzzing around your picnic. What do you do? | Stay calm and move slowly away | Swat it hard with a magazine | Wave your arms to scare it off | Shout and run in circles
exercise: A child in Australia is bitten by a spider. What should they do? | Keep still and tell an adult | Run home as fast as they can | Squeeze the bite to get the poison out | Ignore it, as no spiders are harmful
exercise: You are stung by a jellyfish at the beach. What is right? | Get out, tell a lifeguard and rinse with seawater | Stay in the water and swim it off until the sting stops hurting | Rinse it at once with fresh tap water | Rub sand hard on the sting
exercise: You brush your leg against stinging nettles. What helps? | Wash the skin and do not rub | Rub it hard to spread it out | Scratch it until it stops | Cover it with more leaves
exercise: A boy in Ohio gets an itchy rash after a hike. What might it be? | Poison ivy, so wash and tell an adult | A sunburn, so put plenty of butter on it to cool it down | Chickenpox, so stay away from water | Nothing, so scratch until it stops
exercise: Your classmate has a peanut allergy. What should you do? | Never share food and wash your hands after peanuts | Give her just a tiny piece to try so she gets used to them | Hide peanuts in her lunch as a joke | Tell her allergies are all in the mind
exercise: A friend with an allergy gets a swollen face and cannot breathe well. What do you do? | Get an adult at once and call the emergency number | Give her a glass of cold milk and wait to see if it goes down | Tell her to lie down and rest | Take her for a walk in fresh air
exercise: Why is it good to know where a friend keeps their auto-injector? | It can save their life in a severe reaction | It is fun to use in the playground on each other | It is a special kind of sweet | It helps them run faster in races
exercise: You have a nut allergy and go to a birthday party. What should you do? | Tell the adults and check food labels | Eat anything that does not look nutty | Eat nothing and say nothing | Take your friend's food instead
exercise: You sneeze and have itchy eyes every spring. What may help? | Close windows on high pollen days and wash your face | Roll about in the long grass every day to get used to the pollen | Open all windows in the morning | Pick lots of flowers for your room
exercise: Your chest feels tight and wheezy because of asthma. What do you do? | Use your reliever inhaler and tell an adult | Run faster and faster to open up your lungs | Lie down flat and hold your breath | Wait quietly and tell no one
exercise: A friend is having an asthma attack. How can you help? | Help them sit up, use their inhaler and fetch an adult | Lay them flat on the floor and give them a big drink of water | Make them breathe into a paper bag | Tell them to stop and calm down
test: Why does running at a wasp or swatting it make things worse? | A frightened wasp is more likely to sting | Wasps cannot see slow people | Swatting makes more wasps come out of the ground | Wasps sting only people who run
test: A girl in Queensland is stung by a box jellyfish. What do lifeguards there use? | Vinegar | Fresh tap water | Hot sand | Lemonade
test: Why must a child with a peanut allergy not share even a crumb? | Even a tiny amount can make them very ill | Sharing any food at all is against the school rules | Peanut crumbs are dirty | Their body needs more peanuts first
test: A boy at a sleepover in Calgary eats a biscuit and his lips swell and he wheezes. What is the right thing? | Get an adult at once and call 911 | Let him sleep and check in the morning | Give him some more biscuits | Wait to see if it gets better
test: Which helps a child with hay fever on a high pollen day? | Keeping windows closed and washing their face after being out | Mowing the lawn in the afternoon | Sleeping with the bedroom window wide open all night to get fresh air | Rubbing their eyes when they itch
test: Why should you always read food labels if you have an allergy? | Labels show if the food contains what you are allergic to | Labels show how tasty the food is going to be before you open it | Labels tell you the price of the food | Labels show who made the food
test: After brushing stinging nettles, a boy rubs his leg hard. Why is that a mistake? | Rubbing spreads the sting further | Rubbing makes the nettles grow back | Rubbing turns the sting into a rash for years | Rubbing pushes the plant into your blood
test: A friend has asthma and her chest gets tight in PE. What should she use? | Her reliever inhaler, as the doctor said | A friend's inhaler of a different colour | A bottle of fizzy drink | Nothing; asthma always goes away alone
test: Which is the best reason to tell an adult about a splinter? | An adult can take it out cleanly so it does not get infected | Splinters always need a trip to the hospital and an operation | Adults collect splinters | A splinter grows deeper every hour
test: wasp buzzing round my head, gonna swat it | Stay calm and move slowly away | Swat it hard with your hand | Wave your arms about fast to scare it off | Blow at it to push it away
test: touched nettles, rubbing it to make it better | Do not rub, wash the skin instead | Rub harder, it spreads the pain out | Rub dirt on it to soothe it | Scratch it until it stops
test: jellyfish got me at the beach, rinse with my water bottle? | No, rinse with seawater and tell a lifeguard | Yes, fresh water is best | Rub it with sand instead | Pee on it to stop the sting
test: party at jacks, im allergic to nuts, do i say anything | Yes, tell the adults there and read labels | No, just avoid things that look nutty | No, it might spoil the party | Only tell if you feel ill
explain: A boy in Ireland with a nut allergy shared a friend's biscuit at a party; within minutes his lips swelled and he struggled to breathe, and his adrenaline pen and an ambulance saved him.
explain: A girl in Georgia left a splinter deep in her palm for a week; the skin around it became infected, and a nurse had to take it out and clean it.
fact: A serious allergic reaction can make it hard to breathe within minutes, and it needs an adrenaline pen and the emergency number at once.
fact: A splinter left in the skin can let germs in and cause an infection.
test: my mate's lips puffing up after the cake n he's wheezing | Get an adult, use his allergy pen if he has one, call the emergency number | Give him water and wait | Tell him to lie down and sleep | Give him more cake to calm it
test: got a nut allergy, can i try just a tiny bit of my friend's choc | No, even a tiny bit can make it hard to breathe | Yes, a small bite is safe | Yes, if you wash it down | Only on a weekend
test: stung by a wasp, now my face is swelling n hard to breathe | Tell an adult, call the emergency number now | Rub mud on it and wait | Sleep it off | Squeeze the sting out and keep playing
test: did u hear a kid at camp got stung by a wasp n his whole body blew up like a balloon, he nearly died, my mate saw it | A severe allergy can be dangerous and needs an adult and the emergency number; the balloon bit is likely blown up | Every wasp sting makes the whole body swell, so it is surely true | Wasp stings are never serious for anyone | He should have swatted the wasp harder first
test: Zoe reckons if u wee on a jellyfish sting it stops it, her brother told her | No, get out, tell a lifeguard and rinse with seawater; the wee trick is a myth | Yes, it is what lifeguards are trained to do | Yes, but only in Australia | Fresh tap water is the real trick, not wee
test: everyone says the new girl is "faking" her peanut allergy for attention, should i join in | No, do not spread it; allergies are real and a tiny bit of peanut can make her very ill | Yes, lots of people fake allergies to get noticed | Yes, but only after testing her with a peanut | Yes, if most of the class already says it
exam: Which of the following measures is appropriate upon the observation of swelling, hives, coughing or difficulty in breathing in a person known to suffer from an allergy? | An adult is summoned at once and the emergency number is telephoned | The person is advised to lie down quietly until the symptoms subside | The person is offered a glass of milk and a small quantity of food | The matter is postponed until the person's parents arrive to collect them
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a splinter ought to be removed, with clean tweezers, by an adult. | A splinter left in the skin may admit germs and occasion an infection | A splinter left in the skin will migrate towards the heart within a day | A splinter left in the skin will be absorbed and turned into new bone | A splinter left in the skin is harmless and is removed only for appearance
exam: In respect of a wasp which approaches a child, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The child remains calm and moves slowly away, as swatting renders a sting more likely | The child swats at the wasp vigorously, as this drives the insect away most quickly | The child runs away screaming, since noise is known to frighten wasps away | The child stands entirely still for one hour until the wasp has departed
exam: Pursuant to an asthma attack suffered by a classmate, which course of action is correct? | The person is sat upright, assisted to use the reliever inhaler, and an adult is fetched | The person is laid flat on the floor and instructed to breathe as quickly as possible | The person is given a drink of fizzy water and told to run in order to recover | The person's inhaler is removed in case it should worsen the attack in progress
